WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:04.870
Spider-Man: Far From Home hasn't yet swung
onto theater screens for most fans to see,

00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:09.349
but the long-awaited Spidey sequel premiered
in Hollywood for industry insiders and critics

00:00:09.349 --> 00:00:10.440
on June 26.

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:15.320
The following day, Marvel lifted its embargo
on Far From Home reviews, and outlets wasted

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:18.260
no time publishing critics' initial reactions.

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:23.560
As of noon Eastern on the day the embargo
lifted, Far From Home shined bright with a

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:25.840
92 percent approval rating on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:29.980
That's well above the threshold needed for
a "Certified Fresh" distinction.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:34.540
For the most part, critics are gushing about
Spider-Man: Far From Home, particularly for

00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:40.030
its quote, "breezily unpredictable blend of
teen romance and superhero action," as Rotten

00:00:40.030 --> 00:00:41.880
Tomatoes itself summarizes it.

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:46.860
Reviewers also love that Far From Home, quote,
"stylishly sets the stage for the next era

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:51.200
of the MCU" by introducing characters including
Jake Gyllenhaal's Mysterio.

00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:56.680
Danielle Solzman of Solzy at the Movies gave
the film 4.5 stars out of 5, writing that

00:00:56.680 --> 00:00:57.180
it:

00:00:57.220 --> 00:01:01.620
"...deals with the Avengers: Endgame fallout
in a way that only this film can [and is]

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:05.480
able to close the chapter on one book while
laying the foundation for another."

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:08.940
GamesRadar critic Jordan Farley called Far
From Home:

00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:13.680
"...another home run for the MCU that puts
Pete's responsibilities in a post-Iron Man

00:01:13.690 --> 00:01:18.440
world front and centre during a rib-tickling
summer romance [...] Whatever you do, don't

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:19.380
skip the credits."

00:01:19.790 --> 00:01:24.360
Writing for Mashable, Alexis Nedd noted that
the Spider-Man sequel wonderfully follows

00:01:24.370 --> 00:01:28.900
up Avengers: Endgame and paves the way for
Holland's Peter Parker to play an even bigger

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:31.540
role in the MCU as Phase 4 begins:

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:35.840
"Far From Home doesn't waste an iota of the
good will Marvel has earned in its previous

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:41.220
offerings every cheeky nod towards Peter's
future in the MCU feels genuine […] Even

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:45.240
though the next phase is still shrouded in
mystery, Spider-Man: Far From Home does a

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:50.180
fantastic job of latching the door that Endgame
closed, while opening a whole new universe

00:01:50.180 --> 00:01:51.860
of possibilities for Marvel."

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:55.340
Like Farley, Nedd touted the importance of
the post-credits scenes.

00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:00.290
The Wrap's Alonso Duralde noted that Far From
Home is, quote, "an entertaining example of

00:02:00.290 --> 00:02:04.979
what we used to call 'a summer movie'" thanks
to its playful romance between Peter Parker

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:05.480
and MJ...

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:06.640
"I'm messing with you.

00:02:08.260 --> 00:02:08.760
Thank you."

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:10.060
"You're welcome."

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:11.440
"You look pretty, too."

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:13.900
...as well as Aunt May and Happy Hogan.

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:15.320
Duralde adds that

00:02:15.330 --> 00:02:20.060
"[It has] stakes that are about personal growth
rather than intergalactic armageddon, and

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:22.600
some satisfying hero-on-villain throwdowns."

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:26.950
David Crow at Den of Geek brought up director
Sam Raimi's original Spider-Man trilogy in

00:02:26.950 --> 00:02:29.650
his review of Far From Home, writing that
the flick is

00:02:29.650 --> 00:02:34.850
"...accepting of Raimi's legacy and incorporates
some of that high-flying iconography instead

00:02:34.850 --> 00:02:35.700
of running from it."

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:40.450
He also gave kudos to Gyllenhaal, whom he
felt played Mysterio with, quote, "wide-eyed

00:02:40.450 --> 00:02:41.340
intensity."

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:46.740
"There's art of me that wants me to tell ya
to just turn around and run away from all

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:47.240
this.

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:50.340
And then there's another part of me that knows
what we're about to fight."

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:55.360
At Forbes, Scott Mendelson wrote that Far
From Home is a, quote, "robust improvement"

00:02:55.370 --> 00:02:56.370
upon Homecoming:

00:02:56.370 --> 00:03:00.980
"At the very least, the film presents a Peter
Parker who is relatively good at his job and

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:05.010
doesn't consistently screw up to the point
where you wish he'd hang up the costume before

00:03:05.010 --> 00:03:09.160
he kills someone [...] That was my biggest
gripe with Homecoming, as the film's 'Peter

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:13.620
has to learn to be a hero worthy of the Avengers'
shtick turned him into an MCU variation on

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:14.840
Curious George.

00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:18.840
[...] Far From Home offers more of what worked
last time and less of what didn't."

00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:23.300
Not everyone felt the same way, though, as
a common criticism of Far From Home is that

00:03:23.320 --> 00:03:27.220
it isn't as sleek as Homecoming, despite being
entertaining in its own right.

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:30.860
USA Today's Brian Truitt was one such critic,
writing:

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:35.300
"Far From Home has a lot more boxes to check
than Homecoming in terms of storytelling,

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:39.480
and while it's always enjoyable, the film
feels unfocused in parts.

00:03:39.580 --> 00:03:44.560
Everything changes when a major turn happens
of the story-exploding, can't-talk-about-it-at-all

00:03:44.569 --> 00:03:49.400
variety and the last half takes on a very
meta vibe with some of the craziest visuals

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:54.099
Marvel has unleashed in a while and a stellar
understanding of what Gyllenhaal can do so

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:55.080
well on screen."

00:03:55.420 --> 00:04:00.180
Spider-Man: Far From Home has pulled in some
sour reviews to go alongside the stellar ones.

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:01.500
"I'm sorry."

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:02.540
"Sorry doesn't cut it."

00:04:02.540 --> 00:04:03.040
"I understand.

00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:05.400
I just wanted to be like you."

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:06.820
"And I wanted you to be better."

00:04:07.020 --> 00:04:11.390
Todd McCarthy at The Hollywood Reporter called
the film "bumpy," with, quote, "hokey and

00:04:11.390 --> 00:04:15.950
ill-judged" special effects, "ill-motivated
and less-than-awesome action set-pieces,"

00:04:15.950 --> 00:04:18.500
and a villain that isn't compelling enough.

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:23.300
The New York Post's Johnny Oleksinski said
that Far From Home's action and logic, quote,

00:04:23.309 --> 00:04:28.369
"fall short of its awesome predecessor," while
Pamella Powell of Reel Honest Reviews kept

00:04:28.369 --> 00:04:32.830
things simple when she called the sequel,
quote, "just another formulaic super hero

00:04:32.830 --> 00:04:33.320
film."

00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:38.920
Even so, the general consensus is that the
flick is impressively charming, fun, funny,

00:04:38.920 --> 00:04:39.900
and totally rad.

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:44.420
Judge for yourselves when Spider-Man: Far
From Home hits theaters July 2.

